<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Normals: where is the normal parallel to a given line (INNODEMS)</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Consider the curve</p>
\[ f(x) = {@fx@}. \]
<p>At one point on this curve the <strong>normal</strong> is parallel to the line \( y = {@gl@} \).</p>
<p>(a) At that point, what must the gradient of the <strong>tangent</strong> be?</p>
<div>\( m_{\text{tangent}} = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p>(b) Find the coordinates of the point.</p>
<div>\( x = \) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>
<div>\( y = \) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>
<p>(c) Find the equation of the normal at this point.</p>
<div>\( y = \) [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]]</div>
<div>[[feedback:prt4]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>(a)</strong> The normal is parallel to \( y = {@gl@} \), so the normal has gradient \( {@nn@} \). The tangent is perpendicular to the normal, so</p>
\[ m_{\text{tangent}} = -\frac{1}{{@nn@}} = {@gg@}. \]
<p><strong>(b)</strong> Solve \( f'(x) = {@gg@} \): since \( f'(x) = {@dfx@} \),</p>
\[ {@dfx@} = {@gg@} \quad\Longrightarrow\quad x = {@aa@}, \]
<p>and \( f({@aa@}) = {@fa@} \). The point is \( ({@aa@},\ {@fa@}) \).</p>
<p><strong>(c)</strong> The normal has gradient \( {@nn@} \) and passes through \( ({@aa@},\ {@fa@}) \):</p>
\[ y = {@nn@}\left(x - ({@aa@})\right) + ({@fa@}) = {@nl@}. \]
<p>A common slip is to set \( f'(x) \) equal to the gradient of the given line. But \( f'(x) \) is the gradient of the <em>tangent</em>, and here it is the <em>normal</em> that is parallel to the line.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>4</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.1</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <idnumber/>
    <stackversion>
      <text>2025010100</text>
    </stackversion>
    <questionvariables>
      <text>/* Normal gradient nn = -1/gg where gg is the tangent gradient.  Choose the point
   aa first and then b so that f'(aa) = 2 aa + bq = gg. */
nn  : rand([1/2, -1/2, 1/3, -1/3, 1/4, -1/4]);
gg  : -1/nn;
aa  : rand_with_prohib(-3, 3, [0]);
bq  : gg - 2*aa;
cq  : rand_with_prohib(-4, 4, [0]);
fx  : x^2 + bq*x + cq;
dfx : diff(fx, x);
fa  : subst(aa, x, fx);
ci  : fa - nn*aa;                         /* intercept of the normal */
kk  : rand_with_prohib(-5, 5, [ci]);      /* intercept of the given line */
gl  : nn*x + kk;
nl  : nn*x + ci;                          /* model normal */
tl  : expand(gg*(x - aa) + fa);           /* tangent at the point */</text>
    </questionvariables>
